  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. It has already run once with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, 14,074 documents)\n- **Time:** the time field is `timestamp`. The data runs from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so part of it is timestamped in the future, as Kibana's sample data usually is.\n- **Response codes:** 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441). The field is stored as text and keyword, not as a number, so 503 is the only 5xx code.\n- **Request fields:** `url`, `request`, `extension` (gz, css, zip, deb, rpm), `bytes` (about 5.7 KB on average), `referer` and `agent`.\n- **Client fields:** `clientip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`, and `machine.os` (an even split across win xp, osx, win 7, win 8 and ios).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-8b9c7d9d Web logs overview\"** (id `75713cca-7fc3-4fc9-bf41-b703b58eaae1`, default time range is the last 7 days)\n- **Top row, four single-number panels:** total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), average response size, and the count of 5xx errors.\n- **Requests over time by response code:** stacked bars.\n- **Top 10 requested URLs.**\n- **Traffic by operating system.**\n- **Bytes transferred over time.**\n\nI ran each chart's query separately and they all return data. I didn't open the dashboard in the browser to check how it looks.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-8b9c7d9d 5xx spike\"** (id `9e5dc1fb-a5fb-4833-867d-b6444bd256b9`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code is between 500 and 599 in the last 5 minutes, based on `timestamp`, and fires when the count is above 10.\n- **No notifications are set up.** When it fires, the alert only shows up in Kibana. If you want an email, Slack or similar message, a connector has to be attached to the rule.",
